WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.350
With sci-fi adventure and cast of cosmic misfits
that includes a talking raccoon and his best

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:09.630
friend, a tree, it’s no surprise that Marvel’s
Guardians of the Galaxy was a big hit for

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.900
kids when it hit theaters.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.040
All the adults who saw it, however, might’ve
noticed a few other things that flew right

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.380
over the younger viewers’ heads.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.260
Destination Earth

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.880
Even though he was taken from Earth as a child
and raised among the outlaws known as the

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.460
Ravagers, one of the key components of Peter
Quill’s character is his intense connection

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.460
to his homeworld.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.380
It’s exemplified through the Awesome Mix
cassette tape, given to him by his mother

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.380
before she died, which he listens to nonstop
to form the film’s soundtrack.

00:00:36.929 --> 00:00:40.800
When we see Peter as an adult, it’s been
26 years since he was abducted into space

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:45.769
by the Ravagers, and he’s still holding
onto the mixtape, his backpack, a Troll doll,

00:00:45.769 --> 00:00:48.039
and the few other mementos he has of his old
home.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:52.659
He’s also a capable pilot who knows his
way around the galaxy and even has his own

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:57.679
spaceship, named after the decidedly Earth-based
celebrity Alyssa Milano, so… he definitely

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.720
tried to go back to Earth at some point, right?

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.120
Maybe not recently, since he seems to have
settled into the life of a space-faring outlaw,

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.820
but it’s hard to imagine a universe in which
Star-Lord didn’t run away from the Ravagers

00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:12.610
and head back home at the first chance he
got, even if it was just to see if the Jackson

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:14.320
5 ever put out another song.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.120
Unfortunately, that’s not a story we’ve
ever seen, unless he spent a few years undercover

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.140
in Pawnee, Indiana.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.880
"There's only one man for the job: Burt Macklin."

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:24.760
Tale of the Tape

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:29.620
Peter's trusty Walkman, and the mixtape contained
within it, are more than just a playlist.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.689
It’s his main attachment to the memory of
his mother, and it became the essential hallmark

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:34.620
of the film, too.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.619
It's embedded in the very fabric of the Guardians
of the Galaxy franchise, so much so that when

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:43.680
he has to escape from space prison, Peter
risks his life and his freedom just to get

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:44.240
the tape back.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.660
And it also represents a genuine miracle in
audio preservation technology.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.640
As anyone who was actually around in the ‘80s
knows, cassette tapes aren’t exactly the

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.240
most durable format, especially if you’re
playing them over and over for, say 26 years.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.939
And yet, Peter’s mixtape and the walkman
he uses to play it are in perfect working

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:06.320
condition after being bounced around the universe
for a quarter of a century.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.700
And that's not the only tape-tech Peter has.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.470
Either Sony has a repair tech in Knowhere,
or Peter just happened to run into the only

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.080
member of the Nova Corps who could hook him
up with a tape deck pulled straight out of

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.680
the dashboard of a custom van.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:20.480
Zero G

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.830
When the team is taken to the Kyln prison,
it's up to Rocket to come up with a way to

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:28.540
get out, and what he comes up with actually
ends up working pretty well.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.360
The escape plan hinges on the team getting
up into the guard tower, turning off the artificial

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.960
gravity in the prison, and then simply flying
away.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:40.280
It's a good idea, but it also involves at
least one very large chunk of luck.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.080
For some reason, when the artificial gravity
goes off, the Kyln guards, many of whom had

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:49.850
previously been holding riot shields and rocket
launchers, just start madly flailing around

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:50.700
as they float.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.900
Not only do they never recover as the Guardians
make their escape, they also drop their weapons.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:00.560
Obviously, suddenly losing gravity would have
an element of surprise, but if you’re going

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.880
to build a super-secure prison in the depths
of space, you’d think training your guards

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.760
to fight in zero G would be one of the first
things you’d do.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.040
The second thing?

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.440
Telling them not to drop their guns at the
first sign of trouble.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.220
The Ravager diet

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:19.020
Peter is abducted from Earth as a boy by the
Ravagers, a group of outlaws with their own

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:23.070
code and system of command who were hired
by Peter's still-mysterious father to bring

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:23.560
him back.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:28.200
Yondu, the leader of this particular faction
of Ravagers, decides to keep Peter and raise

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.400
him like a murderous, outer space Mary Poppins.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.240
“I’m Mary Poppins, y’all!”

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.900
Rather than dosing his medicine with a spoonful
of sugar, though, Yondu keeps young Peter

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.040
in line by reminding him that he saved him
more than once from being eaten by his fellow

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:43.540
Ravagers.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.980
They seem pretty eager to find out what creatures
from this distant planet called Earth taste

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:48.480
like.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.340
"When I picked you up on Terra…"

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.040
"Picked me up."

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:52.040
"...these boys of mine wanted to eat you."

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:52.840
"Yeah?"

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.180
"They ain't never tasted any Terran before.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:55.380
I stopped 'em!"

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.880
So are Ravagers space cannibals, or do they
just consider human beings to be livestock?

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:04.700
They’re made up of all manner of alien species,
so if they were that willing to drop a clearly

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.950
sentient Peter Quill onto their plates at
lunchtime, have they also eaten members of

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.360
each others’ species, too?

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.940
That could make for some pretty awkward conversation
around the table, but the movie never bothers

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.660
to answer this question, even though we’re
hungry for answers.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.640
Love and the single alien

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:24.880
Star-Lord definitely fits into the "lovable
scoundrel" category, and apparently that includes

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.500
all definitions of “lovable.”

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:31.200
He apparently has a tendency toward meaningless
flings with all manner of alien species.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.870
He has a woman, whose name he's forgotten,
in his ship when we first meet him, he hits

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:40.850
on Gamora when he sees her, and later he tells
Drax various stories about alien women who've

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:45.120
attacked him after he romanced his way out
of, and into, plenty of trouble.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:49.080
If you’ve ever watched Star Trek and seen
Captain Kirk’s attitude towards the prime

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.680
directive when it comes to green women, you
already know this isn’t exactly a new element

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:54.440
for sci-fi.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:59.620
Still, we have to ask: do all humanoid-looking
aliens have compatible reproductive organs?

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.880
The only elaboration we get from Peter is
a story about flirting with a woman who had

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.500
"tentacles and needles for teeth."

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.690
He says he only spent time with her in a quest
for information, but what would have happened

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:11.820
if he'd taken that interaction to the next
level?

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.780
Maybe we're better off not knowing.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.800
Lazy, Lazy Thanos

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.389
After Avengers: Infinity War, we all know
Thanos as a supervillain who could be not

00:05:20.389 --> 00:05:23.720
just a mastermind, but a punishing and relentless
force.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.520
Before Infinity War, though, his major abilities
seem less built around fistfighting gods,

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.660
and more about delegating tasks to lesser
villains.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.740
This is especially evident in Guardians of
the Galaxy.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:40.219
As he did in The Avengers, Thanos sends someone
else, Ronan the Accuser, to obtain an Infinity

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:41.080
Stone for him.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:45.550
Rather than doing anything himself, he spends
all of his time seated on a floating throne

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:50.169
in space, hurling threats and giving orders
but never actually showing the audience why

00:05:50.169 --> 00:05:51.520
he's so dangerous.

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:55.980
It definitely works in the context of the
larger MCU, but if you were to watch this

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:59.849
film on its own, you'd essentially just be
taking everyone at their word that Thanos

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:00.580
is a bad dude.

00:06:00.849 --> 00:06:05.040
Here, he's basically a guy who tells everyone
else to go do something for him while he kicks

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.020
back and gets irritated when they fail.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.460
No wonder Ronan decided to strike out on his
own when he realized he had an Infinity Stone

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:12.320
in his possession.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:14.280
Mint condition

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.500
When the Guardians realize they need to get
rid of the Orb before it falls into the wrong

00:06:18.509 --> 00:06:23.779
hands, Gamora's plan is to sell it to the
Collector, an ancient and powerful being who…

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.080
well, collects things.

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:29.800
If they give it to him, she reasons, they
can both no longer be responsible for it and

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:30.300
get paid.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:34.960
The Collector is happy to oblige, but while
the group is in his home he notices something

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.820
else he'd like to buy: Groot.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.699
"You must allow me to pay you now so that
I may own your carcass… at the moment of

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:42.540
your death, of course."

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:46.900
While it's certainly nice of him to pay up
front and wait for Groot to live a full life,

00:06:46.909 --> 00:06:50.840
the film also makes it pretty clear that the
Collector has no such scruples about other

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.340
specimens in his collection.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:57.689
Numerous living, seemingly sentient things
are visible in his display cases, including

00:06:57.689 --> 00:07:00.120
Marvel’s own Cosmo the Space Dog and Howard
the Duck.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.659
Here’s hoping they were offered a better
deal than the one Groot was going to get.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:05.460
You know how to whistle, don’t you?

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.189
Of all the interesting space technology on
display in Guardians of the Galaxy, there’s

00:07:10.189 --> 00:07:12.740
nothing more intriguing than Yondu’s Yaka
Arrow.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:17.120
Lifted straight from the comic page, the incredible
sharp and fast weapon is controlled through

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.539
whistling and a technological fin on Yondu’s
head, and it’s powerful enough to slaughter

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:23.860
a whole group of enemies with a simple melody.

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:28.640
It's a neat piece of gear, and while it's
certainly very effective in its current form,

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:32.380
it's hard to watch the Yaka Arrow in action
and wonder why Yondu isn't thinking bigger.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.059
Sure, the Arrow is elegant, but what about
a rocket launcher that works the same way,

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:38.860
or a drone, or a whole ship?

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:44.120
Perhaps it requires more mental energy and
control than Yondu's whistle can summon, or

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:47.629
maybe it’s just limited by being in range
of the whistle’s sound, meaning that he

00:07:47.629 --> 00:07:51.219
couldn’t fly the arrow through the silent
depths of space, but it seems like they might

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:52.440
be shooting pretty low.

00:07:53.069 --> 00:07:54.240
Even though it's remarkably effective.

